          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Lorck directly avows that he loves Constance and immediately emphasizes the seriousness of the avowal. Its dominant force is presenting his emotional state as true, regardless of whether that state is sincere or properly characterized as love.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig, Constance  det er Alvor\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The focal expression itself does not undertake a specified obligation or commitment; it is an avowal made during a courtship appeal. Lorck's adjacent offer of himself and request for acceptance constitute a separate relational undertaking and must not be transferred onto the focal words.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg giver dig mig selv, mit Liv, min Sjæl, -\\nvil du ikke ta imod det?\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.92,\"reason\":\"Although Lorck is emotionally agitated, the focal expression is a deliberate, explicit assertion reinforced as serious, not a reflex-like or uncontrolled affective discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig, Constance  det er Alvor,\\nganske anderledes end jeg selv endog vidste.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"No core force beyond the avowal is needed to characterize the focal expression. Its persuasive role in seeking acceptance is contextual rather than a distinct residual mode.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"After Constance rejects Lorck's advances and asks him to stop, Lorck confronts her and repeats his declaration of love, insists that it is serious, offers her himself, his life, and his soul, and asks whether she will accept. Constance explicitly refuses and retreats from him.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"In the surrounding turn, Lorck undertakes or offers to give Constance himself, his life, and his soul, though the practical terms are unspecified. He asks her to accept this offer, and she rejects it with “Nej, uf nej”. That undertaking belongs to the neighbouring language, not to “Jeg elsker dig” by itself.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied source explicitly identifies Amalie Skram's novel Constance Ring.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage supplies strong evidence of Lorck's passion or infatuation, including his prior account of a year-long attachment and visible agitation, but it cannot conclusively settle his sincerity or whether his possessive and coercive conduct should count as genuine love. The undertaking's concrete terms are also unspecified.\"}"
